<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lihyan</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 65 BCE (approx.) · capital Dedan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Lihyanite kingdom of al-Ula, whose kings taxed the incense road through the Hejaz and carved monumental statues at Dedan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lihyan succeeded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dedan">Kingdom of Dedan</a> at the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alula">al-Ula</a>, probably in the late 6th or 5th century BCE, after the Babylonian occupation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a> ended. Its kings ruled from Dedan and drew their wealth from caravans carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> from south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dadanitic inscriptions record a succession of Lihyanite kings, temple offerings and taxes paid to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhu-ghabat">Dhu Ghabat</a>. Large sandstone statues of kings and nobles from the sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khuraybah">al-Khuraybah</a> show contact with Egyptian and Hellenistic art. Minaean traders from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-main">Kingdom of Ma'in</a> lived alongside the local population and kept their own officials.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lihyan declined as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nabataean-kingdom">Nabataean Kingdom</a> extended its control over the caravan trade and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hegra">Hegra</a> (Mada'in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a>) to the north. By the 1st century BCE its independence had ended, though the dynasty's exact fate is uncertain. The Lion Tombs and statues at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alula">al-Ula</a> are among the region's best-known monuments.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 500 BCE – 65 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:49.75%;width:8.66%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 65 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dedan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism (Dhu Ghabat)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dadanitic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lihyan, Lihyanites</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-282&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lihyan" aria-label="Kingdom of Lihyan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5680 1201 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lihyan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6000 1457L6000 1457M6000 1457L5919 1327"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6000 1457h0M5919 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6000 1457h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6000 1457h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dedan. <a href="/atlas?year=-282&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lihyan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
